Subject: Disable JavaScript optimization through constant tier flags

Base: rusty_v8 v0.106.0, V8 12.9.202.13
(V8 revision 245ce17ed8483e6bc3de7f668635f46c5bfdd8a0).
Requires v8_enable_maglev=false and v8_enable_sparkplug=false in build.sh.

Moonrun executes Wasm but retains JavaScript parsing, Ignition and Inspector
for embedding and debugging. Make the JavaScript flags turbofan, its opt alias
and turboshaft_from_maglev read-only false. V8 declares read-only flags as
compile-time constants, so ordinary C++ optimization can remove the call from
ShouldOptimize/GetOrCompileOptimized into the JavaScript optimizing job.
Final-link dead stripping can then discard the unreachable job and compiler
passes. Runtime command-line flags alone would leave that call reachable.

Keep the compiler implementations and GN source lists intact. Unused code may
remain in the distributed archive; this patch targets the linked executable's
size. It does not require C++ LTO or remove that code's compilation cost.
Consumers need dead stripping enabled in their final link. Retaining all
implementations also lets linkers resolve symbols before discarding sections.

Keep V8_ENABLE_TURBOFAN and V8_ENABLE_TURBOFAN_BOOL unchanged: they describe
shared capabilities needed by Wasm, host-call wrappers and snapshot builtin
generation. Wasm's Liftoff, TurboFan and Turboshaft paths remain. JavaScript
executes in Ignition, and runtime flags cannot enable its optimizing tiers.
RegExp, the JavaScript parser, Inspector and the Rust binding API remain.

Validation: the standalone rusty_v8 consumer checks disabled JS tiers and
numeric Wasm/WasmGC through baseline and optimizing compilation, with GC
references live across a native host call. This is a runtime smoke test, not
an Inspector protocol suite. Check final-binary size and retained symbols on
each linker; passing runtime tests alone does not establish dead stripping.

On upgrade, recheck the read-only flag expansion, implications, JS tier
scheduling, job-factory callers and Maglev/Turboshaft frontend callers. New
references can keep unused code live despite these flags. Do not disable the
shared TurboFan build capability as a substitute. Upstream entry path:
https://github.com/denoland/v8/blob/245ce17ed8483e6bc3de7f668635f46c5bfdd8a0/src/codegen/compiler.cc

--- a/v8/src/flags/flag-definitions.h
+++ b/v8/src/flags/flag-definitions.h
@@ -1120,10 +1120,12 @@
 // Flags for TurboFan.
 #ifdef V8_ENABLE_TURBOFAN
 #define V8_ENABLE_TURBOFAN_BOOL true
-DEFINE_BOOL(turbofan, true, "use the Turbofan optimizing compiler")
+// Keep the shared compiler enabled for Wasm and builtin generation, but do
+// not schedule JavaScript optimization jobs in this Wasm runtime.
+DEFINE_BOOL_READONLY(turbofan, false, "JavaScript optimization is disabled")
 // TODO(leszeks): Temporary alias until we make sure all our infra is passing
 // --turbofan instead of --opt.
-DEFINE_ALIAS_BOOL(opt, turbofan)
+DEFINE_BOOL_READONLY(opt, false, "JavaScript optimization is disabled")
 #else
 #define V8_ENABLE_TURBOFAN_BOOL false
 DEFINE_BOOL_READONLY(turbofan, false, "use the Turbofan optimizing compiler")
@@ -1422,8 +1424,8 @@
 DEFINE_BOOL(turboshaft_wasm_instruction_selection_staged, false,
             "run instruction selection on Turboshaft IR directly for wasm, on "
             "architectures where we are staging the feature")
-DEFINE_EXPERIMENTAL_FEATURE(turboshaft_from_maglev,
-                            "build the Turboshaft graph from Maglev")
+DEFINE_BOOL_READONLY(turboshaft_from_maglev, false,
+                     "the JavaScript Maglev frontend is disabled")
 // inline_api_calls are not supported by the Turboshaft->Maglev translation.
 DEFINE_NEG_IMPLICATION(turboshaft_from_maglev, maglev_inline_api_calls)
 
